This is the last capstone challenge for the SOC 1 pathway! 

Situation: The threat actor Boogeyman was able to infilitrate the organization through another phishing email. I have access to logs through Elastic Stack (ELK).

We were also given an estimate of the date range, so that helped narrow down the amount of logs that had to be looked through. 

To begin, I tried to get more information about the initial payload. By looking up the name of the file in the logs, I was able to find the earliest event with a PID of 6392.

By looking at the other processes under the payload name, I saw that the attacker tried to copy the the file to another location as well. In another entry, it was shown to have successfully run with the rundll32 executable.

Some more information provided was that there was a persistance mechanism initiated by the payload, so I looked for rundll32 scheduled tasks. It was also shared that the file execution created a C2 connection. 

To find the IP address information for this, I queried for powershell in ELK. Once that was done, I backtracked again. The next task shared that the administrator access was used. By looking up the name of the scheduled task again, I was able to find the process the attacker used for privilege escalation.

It was also shared that the attacker tried to credential dump using github. After looking that up in the logs, I was able to discover the exact link that was used to download the credential dumping tool.

In the next task, it was shared that the attacker did successfully use credentials to access another machine. In the search bar, I queried again for the file downloaded as well as user*. With this, I was able to find the credentials the attacker used.

The next step was going further down the logs, I had to find a file that the attacker tried to enumerate using the stolen credentials. This one, I did get stuck on so I had to look it up. However, in my search for the file, I stumbled across another set of credentials that were used. 
This time, it also showed the host name of the computer that was used. 

Next, I tried to find the parent process of the malicious command beind used. I started to do this by filtering for event.code=1 and the hostname i had just found. In that same search result, I also found the username of the newly dumped credentials.
For some reason, I couldn't find the hash even though I used appropriate filter, but I could have missed it, this shows how helpful automation and paying close attention matters.

There was also another 3rd account that was used. I found it by filtering for the parent command lines with event.code=1 and the host name. 

The last task is to find the link for another file the attacker tried to download. This one I had to look up a hint for the filter since there are so many logs. The one that worked the fastest was searching http and filtering out the hostname and the event code.

This capstone challenge was hard but interesting. The most difficult part by far was deciding what keywords and filters to use so that it decreased the amount of logs I had to search through. I will definitely have to do more practice with the log filters.
